WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:21.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Chris Birube, sitting in this week for Roman Mars. In Woonsocket, Rhode Island, there's one surefire way to get yourself clocked as an out-of-towner. Is it Woonsocket? Woon. Woonsocket. Woonsocket. Yep. No double O. No woo. No woo.
这里是《99% Invisible》。我是克里斯·比鲁贝，本周代班罗曼·马尔斯。在罗德岛州的文索基特，有一个万无一失的方法能让你被一眼认出是外地人。是文索基特（Woonsocket）吗？Woon。Woonsocket。Woonsocket。没错。没有双O。没有woo。没有woo。

2
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:51.000
No woo. No woo! No woo! This past spring, producer Jason DeLeon drove to, uh, One Socket, which is just a few miles up the road from where he lives. And in One Socket, I met up with Jean Michonne. Jean is Native American and French-Canadian. So technically, her name is... Jean. God's gift to man. That's what it means. Really? Yeah, my mother had a sense of humor. Pronunciation aside, everyone calls her Jean.
没有woo。没有woo！没有woo！今年春天，制片人杰森·德莱昂开车去了，呃，One Socket，离他住的地方只有几英里远。在One Socket，我见到了让·米雄。让是美洲原住民和法裔加拿大人。所以严格来说，她的名字是……让（Jean）。上帝赐予人类的礼物。这就是它的意思。真的吗？是的，我母亲很有幽默感。发音先不谈，大家都叫她让。

3
00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:17.000
And Jean is third generation one socket. She grew up there in the 1970s. And back then, she said, the city had some stuff going for it. There were places to hang out, like the local movie theater, or the skating rink or the arcade. And because she loved to cook, Jean remembers always jumping at the chance to go with her mom to the grocery store. And that was the biggest thing ever. Woo, let's go. We're going shopping with mom.
让是第三代One Socket人。她在20世纪70年代在那里长大。她说，那时候，这座城市还是有些可取之处的。有很多消遣的地方，比如当地的电影院、溜冰场或游戏厅。因为她喜欢做饭，让记得总是抢着跟妈妈去杂货店。那可是天大的事。喔，走吧。我们要跟妈妈去购物了。

4
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Woohoo. No, you're not getting nothing. At the time, Jean's family had a lot of options for grocers. There were small, locally owned stores like Fernandez Produce and Big D's. The city also had supermarkets, from regional chains like Star Market and Almax. The way some of these local shops bag groceries always mesmerized young Jean. After ringing them up, a cashier would place the groceries in a box on a giant conveyor belt which would crank out the side of the building. And everybody knew that you park there and then it'll come out to you.
喔呼。不，你什么也得不到。当时，让的家人有很多杂货店可选。有像Fernandez Produce和Big D's这样的小型本地商店。城市里也有超市，来自像Star Market和Almax这样的区域连锁店。这些本地商店装杂货的方式总是让年幼的让着迷。结账后，收银员会把杂货放进一个巨大的传送带上的盒子里，传送带会伸出建筑外。大家都知道，你把车停在那儿，东西就会送到你车边。

5
00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:25.000
You know, dad would wait in the car while my kids were shopping, throwing whatever in the boxes. As the years went on, Wendsocket's economy took a turn for the worse. For decades, factory jobs had employed a lot of the city. But those jobs left, and pretty soon, the local movie theater closed. So did the skating rink and the arcade. Then the grocery stores started disappearing. We used to have Star Market, which was always packed on Diamond Hill, and then that one left. One by one, local grocers shut their doors
你知道，爸爸会在车里等，而我们孩子在购物，把东西随便往盒子里扔。随着岁月流逝，文索基特的经济每况愈下。几十年来，工厂工作养活了这座城市的大部分人。但这些工作岗位流失了，很快，当地的电影院关门了。溜冰场和游戏厅也关了。接着，杂货店开始消失。我们以前有Star Market，在Diamond Hill那家总是挤满了人，后来那家也关了。当地的杂货店一家接一家地关门，

6
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:52.000
and never reopened. The decline was so dramatic that today in Woonsocket, a city of 45,000, there's only one grocery store, a Price Right out on the edge of town. It's sad that people don't have other choices to make. And I'm not knocking Price Right, but when you have no choice, but to go to one place, it's like that's the oasis in the desert.
再也没有重开。衰落是如此剧烈，以至于今天在拥有4.5万人口的文索基特，只剩下一家杂货店，位于城郊的Price Right。人们没有其他选择，这很可悲。我并不是在贬低Price Right，但当你别无选择，只能去一个地方时，它就像沙漠中的绿洲。

7
00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:42.000
This is the only spot that they can go to get refreshed is at this price right. Woonsocket is what's known as a food desert. These are low-income communities where it's hard to find groceries. According to the USDA, in a city like Woonsocket, that means lots of residents live more than a mile from the nearest grocery store. And while a mile in a city may not sound like much, according to the latest census information, nearly 20% of Woonsocket's residents don't have access to a vehicle. Pair that with unreliable public transportation, and suddenly a simple task like grabbing groceries can become a real struggle. On its face, the story of how Woonsocket turned into a food desert seems pretty straightforward.
这是他们唯一能去补充物资的地方，就是这家Price Right。文索基特被称为“食物沙漠”。这些是低收入社区，很难买到杂货。根据美国农业部的数据，在像文索基特这样的城市，这意味着许多居民距离最近的杂货店超过一英里。虽然在城市里一英里听起来不算什么，但根据最新的人口普查信息，近20%的文索基特居民没有私家车。再加上不可靠的公共交通，突然间，买杂货这样一件简单的事情就可能变成真正的挣扎。表面上看，文索基特变成食物沙漠的故事似乎很简单。

8
00:03:42.000 --> 00:04:14.000
The city fell on hard times, then businesses abandoned ship, and now it's harder to get basic things like groceries. But the actual story of how when socket and places like it became food deserts is more complicated than that. The common assumption about why food deserts exist, both in the academic literature and among policymakers, is that these are communities that can't support a supermarket. You know, they're either too poor or too small to generate enough spending on groceries and therefore can't support a supermarket.
城市陷入困境，企业弃船而去，现在获取杂货等基本物资变得更难了。但关于文索基特和类似地方如何变成食物沙漠的真实故事要复杂得多。学术文献和政策制定者对食物沙漠存在的原因有一个普遍假设，即这些社区无法支撑一家超市。你知道，它们要么太穷，要么太小，无法产生足够的杂货消费，因此无法支撑一家超市。

9
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Stacy Mitchell is a food researcher and co-executive director at the Institute for Local Self-Reliance. And she thinks this general understanding of food deserts misses the bigger picture. Food deserts didn't used to exist. You know, poverty has been with us forever. But food deserts only arose in the late 1980s. And so this explanation that we have that food deserts are really kind of like there's something wrong with the community, like the community itself is deficient in some way and therefore can't support a supermarket.
斯泰西·米切尔是一位食品研究员，也是地方自力更生研究所的联合执行主任。她认为这种对食物沙漠的普遍理解忽略了更大的图景。食物沙漠过去并不存在。你知道，贫困一直伴随着我们。但食物沙漠直到20世纪80年代末才出现。所以我们这种解释，即食物沙漠是因为社区本身出了问题，比如社区本身在某种程度上存在缺陷，因此无法支撑一家超市，

10
00:04:49.000 --> 00:05:22.000
That's not actually why we have food deserts. Everybody eats, you know, poor neighborhoods spend a lot of money on grocery stores. It's not that there's not enough spending in these neighborhoods. Like something else happened. It's easy to think of food deserts as a natural consequence of poverty. But in communities like Woonsocket, grocery stores stuck around well into the 1990s, long after the factories closed and the city's fortunes faded. Stacey Mitchell says the real reason these food deserts emerged all across the country
这其实并不是我们有食物沙漠的原因。每个人都要吃饭，你知道，贫困社区在杂货店上花了很多钱。并不是这些社区的消费不足。而是发生了别的事情。人们很容易把食物沙漠看作贫困的自然结果。但在像文索基特这样的社区，杂货店一直开到了20世纪90年代，远在工厂倒闭和城市财富消退之后。斯泰西·米切尔说，这些食物沙漠在全国各地出现的真正原因，

11
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:54.000
is because of a decades-long battle over one policy. The story of this policy fight starts with the rise of one of America's first national grocery chains, the Great Atlantic and Pacific Tea Company, better known as AMP. We watch our P's and Q's and A&P. We know you'll be back. For these little green guys. They're our specials of the week. A&P started as a tea company in lower Manhattan,
是因为一场围绕一项政策持续了几十年的斗争。这场政策斗争的故事始于美国第一批全国性连锁杂货店之一——大西洋和太平洋茶叶公司（Great Atlantic and Pacific Tea Company），即众所周知的A&P的崛起。我们注意我们的P和Q，还有A&P。我们知道你会回来的。为了这些绿色小家伙。它们是我们本周的特价商品。A&P最初是曼哈顿下城的一家茶叶公司，

12
00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:25.000
but at the turn of the 20th century, the retailer expanded into the grocery business. At the time, the industry was mostly composed of small independent neighborhood grocers. These mom-and-pop shops would often take orders by phone and then deliver them to your house. They also operated on a credit system, so instead of settling the bill with every purchase, customers would just pay a tab at the end of the month. However, deliveries can get expensive, and operating on a credit system exposed these
但在20世纪之交，这家零售商扩展到了杂货业务。当时，该行业主要由小型独立社区杂货商组成。这些夫妻店通常通过电话接单，然后送到你家。他们还实行信用制度，所以顾客不用每次购买都结账，而是在月底支付账单。然而，送货成本很高，而且实行信用制度使这些

13
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:55.000
grocers to big losses. Sometimes people just didn't pay up. So to cover shortfalls, they'd often mark up their prices. But with the arrival of AMP came a new business model. AMP did away with deliveries and purchases on credit. Instead, at your local A&P, customers would approach the counter and pay on the spot with cash. They also cut costs by vertically integrating their business.
杂货商面临巨大损失。有时人们就是不付钱。为了弥补亏空，他们通常会提高价格。但随着A&P的到来，出现了一种新的商业模式。A&P取消了送货和赊账购买。相反，在当地的A&P，顾客走到柜台前，用现金当场支付。他们还通过垂直整合业务来削减成本。

14
00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:36.000
The company owned manufacturing facilities, which produced A&P branded products, which they sold in A&P stores, all of which helped them offer the lowest prices in town. Prioritizing low prices is a pretty simple change, but it was existential for all of those mom and pop shops. There's a lot of alarm across the country about AMP. You know, they're opening all of these stores, they're putting lots of local grocers out of business and really just taking over the market very rapidly. By the early 1930s, AMP had expanded from grocery stores in the New York metro area
该公司拥有生产设施，生产A&P品牌的商品，并在A&P商店销售，所有这些都帮助他们提供了城里最低的价格。优先考虑低价是一个非常简单的改变，但对所有那些夫妻店来说却是生死攸关的。全国各地对A&P都有很多恐慌。你知道，他们开了这么多店，让很多当地杂货商倒闭，而且确实在非常迅速地接管市场。到20世纪30年代初，A&P已从纽约大都会区的杂货店扩展到

15
00:07:36.000 --> 00:08:20.000
to more than 15,000 stores nationwide. While other chain stores existed, A&P perfected the model. Even in places like Winsocket, A&P had multiple stores throughout the city. And with all that growth, A&P achieved something no retailer in America had ever done before. It produced more than a billion dollars in sales. And the company used all this money and power as a weapon. In negotiations with suppliers, A&P knew that it had the upper hand. And so the company leveraged its size not only to get the very best deals, but to make life harder on their competition.
全国超过1.5万家门店。虽然其他连锁店也存在，但A&P完善了这个模式。即使在像文索基特这样的地方，A&P在全城也有多家门店。随着所有这些增长，A&P实现了美国零售商从未做到的事情。它的销售额超过了10亿美元。该公司利用所有的金钱和权力作为武器。在与供应商的谈判中，A&P知道自己占据上风。因此，该公司利用其规模不仅获得了最好的交易，还让竞争对手的日子更难过。

16
00:08:20.000 --> 00:09:00.000
The reason that A&P is growing so rapidly is that it's squeezing suppliers. It's going to suppliers and saying, we're your biggest customer and we want you to give us big discounts and other favors while raising the prices that you're charging our competitor down the street. This is what's known as the waterbed effect. When suppliers charged AMP less for its products, they made up for it by charging more to the small neighborhood grocers. So like a waterbed, if you sit on one side, the other side goes up. So in effect, what drives AMP's growth is that it has this kind of leverage that comes purely from its size.
A&P之所以增长如此迅速，是因为它在挤压供应商。它去找供应商说，我们是你最大的客户，我们希望你给我们大折扣和其他优惠，同时提高你向街对面竞争对手收取的价格。这就是所谓的“水床效应”。当供应商向A&P收取的费用减少时，他们通过向小型社区杂货商收取更多费用来弥补。就像水床一样，如果你坐在这一边，另一边就会鼓起来。所以实际上，推动A&P增长的是它纯粹因规模而产生的这种杠杆作用。

17
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:41.000
In the 1930s, AMP was viewed by many as a monopoly, and its size was a huge issue in the country. The company was known to blacklist suppliers who didn't bend to their will. It also extracted big fees from farmers and manufacturers to carry their products. From the federal government all the way down to high school debate classes, people argued about AMP and how big is too big. AMP is like changing the fabric of neighborhoods and of towns. It's coming in and running out of business, a lot of local businesses. So there's this big backlash all across the country.
在20世纪30年代，A&P被许多人视为垄断企业，其规模是国内的一个巨大问题。众所周知，该公司会将不屈服于其意志的供应商列入黑名单。它还向农民和制造商收取高额费用以销售他们的产品。从联邦政府一直到高中辩论课，人们都在争论A&P以及多大才算太大。A&P正在改变社区和城镇的结构。它进来并让许多当地企业倒闭。所以全国各地都有这种巨大的反弹。

18
00:09:41.000 --> 00:10:49.000
This pushback against AMP was part of a wider movement against chain stores in general. In small towns throughout the Midwest and the South, big chains threatened local businesses. And because AMP was the biggest chain store, it became the target of communities nationwide. Municipal governments tried to help small local grocers by taxing AMP. Some even passed laws to limit the number of stores it could open in a county. In Shreveport, Louisiana, a local radio station ran a whole program focused on fighting chain stores. A musician named Andrew Jenkins had a message for communities facing a takeover by the likes of AMP. when the hometown merges before it is too late.
这种对A&P的抵制是针对连锁店的更广泛运动的一部分。在中西部和南部的城镇里，大型连锁店威胁到了当地企业。由于A&P是最大的连锁店，它成为了全国社区的目标。市政当局试图通过对A&P征税来帮助小型本地杂货商。有些人甚至通过法律限制它在一个县内可以开设的门店数量。在路易斯安那州的什里夫波特，当地一家广播电台制作了一个专门针对打击连锁店的节目。一位名叫安德鲁·詹金斯的音乐家向面临A&P式接管的社区传达了一个信息：在家乡合并之前，为时未晚。

19
00:10:49.000 --> 00:11:14.000
In the 1930s, the anger with chain stores was also part of a broader frustration with the economy. Many people felt the system just wasn't serving the working class. After all, A&P crossed a billion dollars in sales during the Great Depression. While the low prices helped cash-strapped Americans, it was notable that A&P's rise coincided with the lowest point in many people's lives.
在20世纪30年代，对连锁店的愤怒也是对经济更广泛挫败感的一部分。许多人觉得这个系统根本没有服务于工人阶级。毕竟，A&P在大萧条期间销售额突破了10亿美元。虽然低价帮助了手头拮据的美国人，但值得注意的是，A&P的崛起恰逢许多人生活的最底层。

20
00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:44.000
Soon, Congress picked up on all this outrage. A&P built up its power during an era of relatively lax antitrust enforcement. But as part of FDR's New Deal, corporate power was under intense scrutiny. And with one new piece of legislation, two lawmakers set their sights on breaking up the power of AMP. So in 1936, they passed a law called the Robinson-Patman Act.
很快，国会注意到了所有这些愤怒。A&P在反垄断执法相对宽松的时代建立了权力。但作为罗斯福“新政”的一部分，企业权力受到了严格审查。通过一项新的立法，两名议员将目光投向了打破A&P的权力。于是，在1936年，他们通过了一项名为《罗宾逊-帕特曼法案》（Robinson-Patman Act）的法律。

21
00:11:44.000 --> 00:12:21.000
And it's an antitrust law, and it outlaws what is known as price discrimination. The Robinson-Patman Act was the brainchild of Senator Joseph Robinson from Arkansas and Congressman Wright Patman from Texas. And it gave the federal government a new tool to rein in monopolistic power. The law was even nicknamed the Anti-ANP Act. For years, AMP pressured suppliers into giving them the best deals. But the Robinson-Patman Act changed that dynamic. It outlawed price discrimination, which means that whatever sweet deal AMP received from a supplier
这是一项反垄断法，它取缔了所谓的“价格歧视”。《罗宾逊-帕特曼法案》是阿肯色州参议员约瑟夫·罗宾逊和德克萨斯州众议员赖特·帕特曼的结晶。它为联邦政府提供了一个遏制垄断权力的新工具。该法案甚至被称为“反A&P法案”。多年来，A&P向供应商施压，要求获得最好的交易。但《罗宾逊-帕特曼法案》改变了这种动态。它取缔了价格歧视，这意味着A&P从供应商那里获得的任何优惠交易，

22
00:12:21.000 --> 00:13:02.000
had to be the same sweet deal offered to other retailers, including small independent grocers. The law makes it illegal for suppliers to provide different prices to different buyers. It also makes it illegal for retailers that have market power to demand special deals, discounts, other kinds of favorable treatment. One big hang up that people had with the Robinson-Patman Act is this idea that it banned bulk discounts. But that's not exactly true. Big chains could still get a lower price for buying in bulk. Other retailers just needed access to the same terms.
都必须同样提供给其他零售商，包括小型独立杂货商。该法律规定供应商向不同买家提供不同价格是非法的。它还规定拥有市场权力的零售商要求特殊交易、折扣或其他形式的优惠待遇是非法的。人们对《罗宾逊-帕特曼法案》的一个大顾虑是它禁止批量折扣。但这并不完全正确。大型连锁店仍然可以因批量购买而获得更低的价格。其他零售商只需要获得相同的条款即可。

23
00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:49.000
The goal was to make retailers compete over things like innovation, product selection, and customer service, rather than using purely market power to shove competition out. A&P tried to persuade the public that its size wasn't a problem. In a magazine profile, the president of the company warned that breaking up their power would actually raise grocery prices. And in one newspaper ad, ANP thumbed its nose at antitrust enforcers by showing a picture of the Empire State Building with a caption that read, It's far too big. It ought to be seven buildings. Still, the Federal Trade Commission and the Department of Justice kept pressuring the company. And after Robinson-Patman went into effect, the changes
目标是让零售商在创新、产品选择和客户服务等方面竞争，而不是纯粹利用市场权力将竞争对手挤出市场。A&P试图说服公众，其规模不是问题。在一份杂志简介中，该公司总裁警告说，打破他们的权力实际上会提高杂货价格。在一则报纸广告中，A&P展示了一张帝国大厦的照片，配文写道：“它太大了。它应该是七座建筑。”以此嘲弄反垄断执法者。尽管如此，联邦贸易委员会和司法部仍不断向该公司施压。在《罗宾逊-帕特曼法案》生效后，

24
00:13:49.000 --> 00:14:21.000
to the grocery business were almost immediate. The law ate into AMP's profits. And within a few years, the company's exponential growth looked a lot more like normal growth. The steep decline of the small neighborhood grocer was headed off. AMP is no longer, you know, sort of aggressively like gobbling up the entire market. They're stopped from doing that, but they're still, you know, a force. The Robinson-Patman Act didn't destroy AMP. It just stopped the company from abusing its
杂货业的变化几乎是立竿见影的。该法律削减了A&P的利润。几年之内，该公司的指数级增长看起来更像是正常增长。小型社区杂货商的急剧衰落得到了遏制。A&P不再像以前那样咄咄逼人地吞噬整个市场。他们被阻止这样做，但他们仍然是一股力量。《罗宾逊-帕特曼法案》并没有摧毁A&P。它只是阻止了该公司滥用其

